   318	### Figure/Table 3
   319	
   320	#### Table 3 + Figure 3a, 3b and 3c
   321	
   322	These three figures and the table were all generated by analyzing the results of our largest benchmarking set with a total of 5000 experiments: `benchmarkingset-rc2-results`.
   323	The benchmarks differ in the **number of states**, **maximum number of outputs** and **number of discarded steps** in traces (i.e. injected glitches).
   324	
   325	The following commands generate the respective table/figures:
   326	
   327	```bash
   328	# count the number of timeouts and pipe to file (-> table3.csv)
   329	python parse_all_results_timeouts.py benchmarkingset-rc2-results > table3.csv
   330	
   331	# generate plot for % solved, plotting max outputs (-> figure3a.png)
   332	python parse_all_results_solve_out.py benchmarkingset-rc2-results
   333	
   334	# generate plot for % solved, plotting percentage of glitches (-> figure3b.png)
   335	python parse_all_results_solve_drop.py benchmarkingset-rc2-results
   336	
   337	# generate plot for avg. solving time, plotting percentage of glitches (-> figure3c.png)
   338	python parse_all_results_time_drop.py benchmarkingset-rc2-results
   339	```
   340	
   341	**Results:**
   342	
   343	* Table 3: `table3.csv`
   344	* Figure 3a: `figure3a.png`
   345	* Figure 3b: `figure3b.png`
   346	* Figure 3c: `figure3c.png`
   347	
   348	#### Figure 3d
   349	
   350	The benchmarking set `benchmarkingset-faults-results` contains the benchmarks and results of injecting **different types of faults** into the traces, instead of only dropping input-output pairs.
   351	
   352	The results can be analyzed with the following command, which will generate the corresponding figure:
   353	
   354	```bash
   355	# generate plot for different faults (-> figure3d.png)
   356	python parse_all_results_faults.py benchmarkingset-faults-results
   357	```
   358	
   359	**Results:**
   360	
   361	* Figure 3d: `figure3d.png`
   362	
   363	#### Figure 3e
   364	
   365	The benchmarking set `benchmarkingset-trace-results` contains the benchmarks and results of solving for  **traces of different length**.
   366	
   367	The analysis of the trace length benchmarks, including drawing the box plot, can be done with:
   368	
   369	```bash
   370	# generate plot for different trace lengths (-> figure3e.png)
   371	python parse_all_results_trace.py benchmarkingset-trace-results
   372	```
   373	
   374	**Results:**
   375	
   376	* Figure 3e: `figure3e.png`
   377	
   378	#### Figure 3f
   379	
   380	The benchmarking set `benchmarkingset-o5-g1` contains random automata and traces for |O| <= 5 and 1% injected glitches. We use this to compare our algorithms performance against the **performance of IO ALERGIA**.
   381	
   382	With the following command we *run* IO ALERGIA against each example in the benchmarking set (with different parameters) and then generate a plot displaying the relative [F1-scores](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/F-score):
   383	
   384	```bash
   385	# run IO ALERGIA and generate plot (-> figure3f.png)
   386	python evaluate_results_alergia_fscore.py benchmarkingset-o5-g1
   387	```
   388	
   389	> Note, this may take a few minutes to run as IO ALERGIA is performed 6 times per example
   390	
   391	**Results:**
   392	
   393	* Figure 3f: `figure3f.png`
